# Google Maps — three keys, one per platform.
#
# Google allows a key exactly one *application* restriction: Websites, or iOS
# apps, or Android apps. An app that runs on all three therefore needs three
# keys. See src/lib/maps/keys.ts for which call uses which.
#
# Every one of these is optional. An unset key degrades to an empty string: the
# map screen reports itself unconfigured and nothing else is affected, so a
# checkout with no .env at all still builds, type-checks, and runs. Set only the
# ones you are building for.
#
# These are PUBLIC_ variables: they are inlined into the client bundle and are
# readable by anyone using the app. That is inherent to client-side Maps keys.
# What protects them is restriction, not secrecy — configure each one in the
# Google Cloud console as described below, and enable only the APIs it needs.

# Restriction: Websites (HTTP referrers)
# APIs: Maps JavaScript API, Places API (New), Geocoding API
#
# Web only, despite the "used everywhere" it may look like. `webKey()` has one
# caller — the JS SDK loader — and places.ts only reaches the SDK on its
# !isNative() branch. On device the map is a native view and autocomplete and
# geocoding go over REST with the *platform* key, so the JS SDK never runs in
# the Capacitor shell. Do NOT add `capacitor://localhost/*` or
# `https://localhost/*` to this key; they were needed before the REST migration
# and are now just extra surface.
#
# Use a DIFFERENT key here than the one Cloudflare Pages builds with, so neither
# list has to carry the other's origins. Same variable name, different value per
# environment — nothing in the code changes.
#
#   local .env (this file, gitignored)   http://localhost:5173/*   (npm run dev)
#                                        http://localhost:4173/*   (npm run preview)
#
#   Cloudflare Pages env var             https://app.bmlt.app/*
#   (set from the cloudflare-pages repo's terraform.tfvars, pushed with
#    ./scripts/set-pages-env.sh — Pages bakes it in at build time)
PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_KEY_WEB=

# Restriction: iOS apps — bundle ID ie.nasouth.bmltsearch
# APIs: Maps SDK for iOS, Places API (New), Geocoding API
# Not only the map SDK: on device this key also signs the REST calls for place
# autocomplete and geocoding, authenticated by X-Ios-Bundle-Identifier rather
# than by a referrer. Restricting it to just the map SDK breaks the search box
# — silently, since a failed autocomplete degrades to an empty suggestion list.
PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_KEY_IOS=

# Restriction: Android apps — package ie.nasouth.bmltsearch + your signing SHA-1
# APIs: Maps SDK for Android, Places API (New), Geocoding API
# As with iOS, this key also signs the REST autocomplete and geocoding calls,
# authenticated by X-Android-Package + X-Android-Cert.
# The Android build ALSO needs this value in the manifest, which Gradle reads
# separately from GOOGLE_MAPS_KEY_ANDROID or android/local.properties. See the
# README — the Maps SDK for Android takes its key from the manifest, not from
# the apiKey passed to GoogleMap.create().
PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_KEY_ANDROID=

# SHA-1 of the certificate that signs your Android build, e.g.
# F0:5A:84:...:B9 (colons optional). Android's key restriction checks package
# name AND signature, and on device the app proves its identity to the Places and
# Geocoding REST endpoints with X-Android-Package + X-Android-Cert headers — it
# cannot read its own signature at runtime, so the value is supplied here.
#
# Google validates this header against the fingerprints registered on the key —
# it cannot check the app's real signature — so ONE registered value works for
# every build type. Use the fingerprint of the keystore CI signs with, and make
# sure that same fingerprint is on the key's Android restrictions.
#
# Getting this wrong fails silently: Places returns
# "Requests from this Android client application are blocked", the app catches
# it, and autocomplete just stops suggesting with no error on screen.
#
#   keytool -list -v -keystore <keystore> -alias <alias>
#
# iOS needs no equivalent: its bundle identifier is static and known.
PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_ANDROID_CERT_SHA1=
